  "overall": "On the 5 non-stiff practical problems at a 65536-cycle budget under m0plus_fast with floor rounding, the best discovered method has lower Q15 error than the best classical anchor on 4 of 5; the median ratio of best-discovered to best-classical Q15 error is 0.475 (below 1.0 favors the discovered methods). On the 3 moderately stiff problems (stiffness ratios 292 to 1030): where both sides finish, the best discovered method is ahead on 1 of 2 with a median error ratio of 0.998; on 1 of 3 no discovered method finishes at all; every one overflows in Q15 while cheap low-stage classical methods complete the run. The pattern is the stability tax of explicit methods: at a fixed cycle budget an expensive high-order tableau takes larger steps, and once the step size times the fast eigenvalue leaves the stability interval the Q15 run overflows, whoever found the tableau. This is the motivating evidence for the epoch-3 implicit (SDIRK) track.",
